<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>8.9.3: Writing an Equation to Represent a Quadratic Function with Only One Solution</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02235</md:content-id>
    <md:title>8.9.3: Writing an Equation to Represent a Quadratic Function with Only One Solution</md:title>
    <md:uuid>af8f103b-35dd-422d-94d6-e2e42484d42e</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <title>Activity </title>
      <para>In previous lessons, we saw that a <term class="glossary-tooltip">quadratic equation</term> can have zero, one, or two solutions.</para>
      <example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>Sketch graphs that represent three <term class="glossary-tooltip">quadratic functions</term>: one that has no zeros, one with one zero, and one with two zeros.</para>
            <para>Write down your answers, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answers:</para>
            <para>No zeros:</para>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_09_11_06_01.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
           </para>
            <para>One zero:</para>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_09_11_06_02.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
           </para>
            <para>Two zeros:</para>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_09_11_06_03.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
           </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
        <item>Graph the function defined by \(f(x) = x^2− 2x + 1\). Examine the \(x\)-intercepts of the graph.</item>
      </list>
      <para>Use the Desmos graphing tool or technology outside the course.</para>
      <iframe src="../../media/desmos/a47b5bb4ba783be55a7067b919f165f14dcc36ce.html" width="500" height="400"/><!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Explain what the \(x\)-intercepts reveal about the function.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Enter your explanation.</para>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>The graph only intersects the \(x\)-axis at one point: \((1, 0)\). The function only has one zero.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Solve \(x^2− 2x + 1 = 0\) by using the factored form and <term class="glossary-tooltip">zero product property</term>. What solutions do you get?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Enter the solutions.</para>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Rewrite the standard form to \((x − 1)(x − 1) = 0\). The solutions are \(x=1\) and \(x=1\). Because the two values are the same, we say that this equation has one solution and that \(x = 1\) is a double root for the equation.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
        <item>Try to find another quadratic function that you think will only have one zero.</item>
      </list>
      <para>Use the Desmos graphing tool or technology outside the course.</para>
      <iframe src="../../media/desmos/a47b5bb4ba783be55a7067b919f165f14dcc36ce.html" width="500" height="400"/><!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Graph it to check your prediction, then enter your final answer.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Enter your answer.</para>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>For example: \(f(x) = (x + 4)(x + 4)\). When graphed, this function only has one zero, at \(x=-4\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <title>Video: Quadratic Equations with Only One Solution</title>
